# Fedora packages that need root or a system service.
#
# This is the dnf equivalent of platform/darwin/Brewfile. Everything else
# belongs in Home Manager (home/), which needs no root.
#
# How it is used:
#   platform/fedora/bootstrap.sh  runs `sudo dnf install` on every name here
#   scripts/doctor                fails if a name here is not installed, and
#                                 fails if dnf shows a hand-installed package
#                                 that is not listed here (drift)
#   scripts/inventory             prints this list
#
# Rules:
#   - one package name per line; blank lines and # comments are ignored
#   - to add a package: add it here, run `just bootstrap <target>`
#   - to remove one: delete it here, then `sudo dnf remove <name>` yourself
#   - never `sudo dnf install` by hand; doctor will report it as drift

# Nix itself: Fedora's own package, not a third-party installer. ./bootstrap
# installs these two before this file can be read; they are listed here so
# doctor and inventory tell the truth about what is on the machine.
nix
nix-daemon

# GUI apps come from the OS's own channel when it has a trustworthy build,
# on both platforms (macOS: platform/darwin/Brewfile); Nix owns the command
# line. Ghostty is the exception and stays on nixpkgs: Fedora has no package
# of its own and the COPRs are community builds. docs/nix.md, "GUI Apps".

# Remote desktop. RustDesk's own rpm from its GitHub release, which its
# docs list first for Fedora. A URL line is installed with dnf like a name;
# bump the version here to update. https://rustdesk.com/docs/en/client/linux/
https://github.com/rustdesk/rustdesk/releases/download/1.4.9/rustdesk-1.4.9-0.x86_64.rpm

# ChatGPT desktop (ChatGPT + Work + Codex in one app; Linux preview, Fedora
# supported). OpenAI's own rpm, package name `chatgpt`. OpenAI publishes only
# a "latest" URL, so unlike RustDesk this is not version-pinned: a bootstrap
# run installs whatever is current. https://openai.com/codex/
https://persistent.oaistatic.com/codex-app-prod/linux/rpm/latest/chatgpt.x86_64.rpm

# Mullvad VPN. Needs a root daemon, so it is a system package: Mullvad's own
# dnf repository, which bootstrap adds. https://mullvad.net/en/download/vpn/linux
mullvad-vpn

# Tailscale. Needs the root daemon tailscaled, so a system package, from
# Tailscale's own dnf repository, which bootstrap adds and which is what
# Tailscale's installer (https://tailscale.com/install.sh) uses on Fedora.
# Fedora's own package exists but lags several releases. docs/tailscale.md.
tailscale

# Network inspection: nmap (who is on the LAN, what ports they expose) and
# Wireshark (packet capture and analysis; tcpdump is in Fedora's base
# install already). System packages rather than Home Manager because both
# need raw sockets: sudo drops ~/.nix-profile/bin from PATH, and Fedora's
# wireshark ships dumpcap with file capabilities plus a `wireshark` group so
# you capture as yourself and the GUI never runs as root (bootstrap adds
# you to the group; log out and in once after). Only scan networks you own.
#
# `wireshark` is the GUI and pulls in `wireshark-cli` (tshark, the same
# dissectors from the terminal, and dumpcap). If the GUI turns out to go
# unused, list `wireshark-cli` here instead and `sudo dnf remove wireshark`.
nmap
wireshark

# Containers: podman, Fedora's own, already on the machine because Fedora
# Workstation's toolbox pulls it in. Listed so the choice is written down
# rather than an accident of the install. No `docker` command here today;
# the servers run Docker CE (fleet, roles/docker) and the Mac Docker
# Desktop. If that changes, docs/containers.md has the switch step by step.
# Never `podman-docker`: it takes /usr/bin/docker and conflicts with every
# real Docker package.
podman
